      "problem": "Consider the equation ${y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}=h.$ For which values of $h$ does this equation have no real solutions?\n\n$h$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3} & =h \\\\ {y}^{2} & =h-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{h-\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ |y| & =\\sqrt{h-\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\nh-\\frac{1}{3}<0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h-\\frac{1}{3} & <0 \\\\ h & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $h$ that is less than $\\frac{1}{3}$ will give an equation with no real solutions.",
